# G079 — Text Based Game (like Utopia)

*v2. See `text-game.md` for prose: room graph as simulation world,
every command is a turn (input-driven ticking), verb-noun parsing
with aliases, items with kind-specific behaviour behind one `use`,
win/lose as post-turn invariants, log as the narrative.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Interactive Agent Training Dungeon

[agent-training-dungeon @policy
    IN ORDER TO
        drive a text-based world from an agent policy
        where each observation feeds a decision and each decision
        advances the world by one turn until a terminal state

    @world <- [tg[new-demo-world]]
    @agent <- [spawn
                {policy: @policy,
                 initial-state: [tg[player-state {world: @world}]]}]

    loop
        @observation <- [tg[describe {world: @world}]]
        @command     <- [agent[decide
                                 {agent: @agent,
                                  observation: @observation}]]
        @result      <- [tg[execute
                             {world: @world, cmd: @command}]]

        @score <- [reward
                    {before: @agent:state,
                     after:  [tg[player-state {world: @world}]]}]
        @agent <- [learn {agent: @agent,
                           reward: @score,
                           observation: @observation}]

        [tg[state {world: @world}]]
            where is not "Playing" -> break
]

---

## Verb Surface

[tg
    new-demo-world
    describe            {world}                  ;; narrative snapshot
    execute             {world, cmd}              ;; -> {kind, events}
    player-state        {world}
    state               {world}                   ;; "Playing" | "Dead" | "Won"
    parse               {input}                   ;; verb-noun w/ aliases
    log                 {world}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: every command is a turn `where` provoking action

[tg[execute {world: @w, cmd: @cmd}]
    IN ORDER TO
        resolve @cmd against the player's room and inventory
        then let the world react (enemies in this room strike back)
        then check win/lose invariants on the resulting state

    phase 1: parse @cmd into verb + noun (with alias normalisation)
    phase 2: dispatch verb on current room + player + inventory
             where verb in ["go", "take", "use", "fight", "drop"]
                 -> provokes (advances @w:turn by 1)
             where verb in ["look", "inventory", "stats"]
                 -> observation only (does not provoke)
    phase 3: for each enemy in player's current room
                 where verb provoked
                 -> enemy strikes; append to @w:log
    phase 4: check end-of-turn invariants
                 where player:hp at most 0       -> state := "Dead"
                 where player:gold at least @w:win-threshold
                                                  -> state := "Won"
    phase 5: commands on terminal state are rejected
]

Observation commands (`look`, `inventory`) don't advance the clock.
Provoking commands do. Same primitive as G078's tick, but the clock
source is user input, not wall-clock.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: item effect dispatched by `where` kind

[tg[use {world: @w, item-id: @id}]
    @item <- @inventory at @id
        where kind is Potion     -> heal @item:heal points
        where kind is Treasure   -> add @item:worth-gold
        where kind is Weapon     -> equip, update damage
        where kind is Key        -> unlock adjacent locked room
    append effect to @w:log
]

One user-facing verb, polymorphic effects by receiver kind. User
doesn't have to know which code path runs; the dispatch is the item's
own concern.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: terminal states as post-turn `where` invariants

After every turn the world checks:
- hp ≤ 0 → Dead (regardless of what caused it)
- gold ≥ win-threshold → Won (regardless of which action added gold)

These are invariants on the state, not side effects of specific verbs.
Combinations the programmer didn't explicitly code still trigger the
correct terminal state — same robustness as SIGSEGV on invalid writes
or FK-constraint rejection in databases.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @w <- [tg[new-demo-world]]

    -> [tg[execute {world: @w, cmd: "go north"}]]
    ;; player moves; turn := 1; room's goblin strikes player

    -> [tg[execute {world: @w, cmd: "look"}]]
    ;; observation, turn unchanged

    -> [tg[execute {world: @w, cmd: "fight goblin"}]]
    ;; combat, goblin possibly dies

    -> [tg[execute {world: @w, cmd: "take gold"}]]
    ;; pick up; win-threshold met → state := Won

    -> [tg[state {world: @w}]]    ;; -> "Won"
    -> [tg[log   {world: @w}]]    ;; -> the full turn-by-turn narrative
]
